WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.320
HBO's "Game of Thrones" prequel series, "House&nbsp;
of the Dragon" is bringing fans back over 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.360
years before the events of the main series. With&nbsp;
a new teaser for this prequel recently released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.360
we've got some questions. For starters, was that&nbsp;
Dragonstone? And what's with that new Stark?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.680
By the time "Game of Thrones" begins, there are no&nbsp;
more folks of Old Valyrian blood left on Westeros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.120
Daenerys and her brother Viserys are wandering&nbsp;
Essos in exile after the events of Robert's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.160
Rebellion. The new "House of the Dragon" trailer&nbsp;
presents a very different state of affairs, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.760
more platinum-headed dragonriders skulking around&nbsp;
than you can shake a flaming sword at. What gives?

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.960
In the deep Westerosi past, long before the&nbsp;
time of the Mad King Aerys and Robert's fateful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.560
rebellion, there were a lot more Valyrians running&nbsp;
around Westeros — and they weren't all Targaryens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.400
either. In the mass exodus from Essos after the&nbsp;
Doom of Valyria, two prominent Valyrian families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.680
actually conquered Westeros — the Targaryens and&nbsp;
the Velaryons, the latter of which has died out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.000
by the time "Game of Thrones" begins. At the time&nbsp;
of "House of the Dragon," however, the Velaryons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.760
are still very much on the scene, and will play a&nbsp;
central role in the Targaryen civil war to come.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.280
Marked by their silver hair, expect&nbsp;
the Velaryons to play the Game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.800
Thrones hard in the new series.&nbsp;
Since there aren't any left by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.400
the time the flagship series starts,&nbsp;
you can imagine how that turns out.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.000
In the new teaser trailer to "House&nbsp;
of The Dragon," audiences witness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.480
some familiar names bending the&nbsp;
knee to King Viserys I Targaryen

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.920
"I, Rickard Stark."

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:32.880
"I, Corlys Valaryon."

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.960
"I, Corbin Baratheon."

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.320
"Thrones" superfans will likely remember that&nbsp;
Eddard Stark's father was named Rickard Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.600
but the Rickard seen in this trailer is alive some&nbsp;
200 years before "Game of Thrones" even starts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.080
so we're talking a totally different Rick here.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.160
Characters in the world of "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
tend to name their children after deceased family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.440
members, and the Targaryens are no different.&nbsp;
King Viserys I Targaryen sits upon the Iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.000
Throne at the start of "House of the Dragon."&nbsp;
Fans who closely recall Season 1 of "Game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.040
Thrones" will note that Daenarys' older brother&nbsp;
Viserys shares the same distinguished moniker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.600
This makes King Viserys I Targaryen&nbsp;
Dany and Viserys' distant ancestor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.320
living some 200 years before any of the&nbsp;
final generation of Targaryens are born.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.560
Targaryen family members have a tendency&nbsp;
to slip into some disturbing behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.000
but it seems Viserys I doesn't fall into that&nbsp;
specific category. The show's official page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.440
describes Viserys I as a kind and decent&nbsp;
king before issuing a stark reminder:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.840
In the world of "Game of Thrones," good&nbsp;
men don't necessarily make for good kings.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.680
"When you play the game of thrones, you&nbsp;
win or you die. There is no middle ground."

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.760
Based on the teaser trailer and what we know about&nbsp;
Westerosi history already, "House of the Dragon"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.200
seems to dive into the conflict that arises as&nbsp;
Viserys I grapples with his brother's ambition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.760
even as he names his first-born child&nbsp;
the heir apparent to the Iron Throne.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.040
In the new trailer for "House of the Dragon," a&nbsp;
familiar and ornate war room in Dragonstone should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.440
have caught the attention of eagle-eyed "Game&nbsp;
of Thrones" fans. This particular meeting spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.040
in "Game of Thrones" hosts Stannis Baratheon and&nbsp;
the Red Priestess Melisandre for several seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.880
but the heavily fortified city is&nbsp;
left vacant after Stannis' defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.600
and is easily reoccupied by Daenerys Targaryen&nbsp;
after she makes her voyage from Essos.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.240
Fans familiar with "Game of Thrones" lore will&nbsp;
remember that — before Robert's Rebellion — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.560
fortress of Dragonstone was part of the&nbsp;
Crownlands under the paramount lordship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.440
of the Targaryen kings. By tradition, the&nbsp;
fortress was held by the Targaryen crown prince,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.200
Rhaegar at the time of Robert's Rebellion. That's&nbsp;
why Daenerys "Stormborn" is born on said stormy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.960
island. She was secreted away to that stronghold,&nbsp;
while her brother was out prosecuting Aerys' war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.280
and fathering Jon Snow with Lyanna&nbsp;
Stark. Stannis is actually the first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.480
and the last, Baratheon to hold Dragonstone.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.520
So, what military mischief might the dragonriders&nbsp;
be planning around the table this go-around?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.760
Notably, the island of Dragonstone is&nbsp;
adjacent to the island of Driftmark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.280
seat of House Velaryon. If a conflict is to&nbsp;
break out between the two Old Valyrian houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.080
these two islands are likely&nbsp;
to be at the center of it.

